  understand what I am before anyone else gets to decide it for me"); Lira's correction on the step ("you're not building it by
  yourself"); "What do you see when you use Iron Skin's pressure read?" — "Something I've never seen before." — the shelf that isn't there.

Physical challenges: the interim bout; Lira vs Wendel; Lira's fragmentless drilling; the baseline chain test.

Development: Brom's arc starts in earnest (four to five beats); Lira (two to three); Vell (one); Dace (one); Coss (one to two). Cael
≈4–5 per 10k. The first third ends inside this movement: keep progression vocabulary high through chapter 20, then let it begin to recede.

## What must remain true / withheld

- The Brom bout does not happen in this movement; end before it (about four days out).
- Brom's read of Cael is absence of a category, never a diagnosis. Nobody suggests Cael takes abilities by watching (Book 3).
- Coss's cutaway names no origin and makes no note; he files nothing until Movement 5.
- Lira is *provisional* Iron-equivalent at most; confirmation bouts come later.
- Velmere stays as written (owner's call). Brom's family does not write to him in this book.
- Bronze-equivalent is the top of the circuit ladder (§11 k). Reader Standard.

## What the author owns

How the Brom cutaway is braided into Cael's chapters; how many of the twelve sessions are dramatized; the reading-room texture; which
interim bout is shown; Lira's fight tactics; the boarding-house evenings. Humor: Brom's economy of speech meeting Lira's. The author may
let Cael and Brom argue over breakfast about latency once they are speaking.

## Where we leave pressure

A scheduled bout against a friend-in-waiting whose Path is a machine for returning force, with a hypothesis (a transition window in the
third exchange) built from two weeks of honest watching — and a plan that locks one of Cael's two fragments away by rule. The main floor
is posted. The district is betting against him at unflattering numbers.

