editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:1:# Chapter 21 — Off Side
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:9:"Yes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:27:"If I'm right. I've seen it once, live." He turned forward again. "So I make him answer hard three times, close together, and put the fourth in the recovery." He moved his finger to the drawing. "Low. Below the knee. That's where he's thinnest anyway. If I'm right about the recovery and right about the knee, the fourth goes into a man who's only a man there, for two beats."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:63:*Against B.: no giving face. Not once. Not on the forearm, not on the chest, not anywhere he can harden. Every hit I make heavier, he sends back heavier. This is not a ceiling. It's a rule.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:73:"And I've got the boy who was there before them," said Cael. "You found him for me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:87:"You're moving it about. I can see the bottom of the plate in exactly the same places." The sister turned to Cael. "And you. They say you've got the big one on the main floor. Tuesday. It's all down the market."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:89:"Yes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:95:"Probably not," said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:117:"You never said," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:151:"She asked me not to watch her," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:159:"No," said Brom. "I can not look at it. Like not listening to the next room."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:161:"Begin," said Vell.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:211:At the end of the fifth they were both breathing like bellows. Lira's upper arm, where the first sequence had caught her, had gone a dark red she would be wearing for a week. She walked to her mark and did not look at Cael. She looked at the floor in front of her, at the place where she would stand, with her lips not moving at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:213:"Begin."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:223:"Called," said Vell. "Hand up. Sixth exchange."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:233:Lira looked past him at the rope. She looked at Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:237:She looked back at Dace.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:239:"Yes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:247:"Nobody." Lira held his eyes. "I watched you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:253:"In the morning," said Vell. "Come and read it in the morning. Not now, with your arm like that and half the room watching you." She looked at Lira over her spectacles. "I want you to read it sitting down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:275:"There's a thing I want to ask you," said Brom. "Not Tuesday."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:277:"Ask."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:279:"What's it for?" He tipped his head back down the hill toward the Ironyard, and seemed to mean all of it at once: the bench, the grey book, the two fingers on the wrist, the column ruled with a straightedge. "The ones I've met who fight like you, it's for something plain. Coin. A number in a book. A father. I've watched you longer than three weeks now. It isn't any of those."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:287:"There's a page in a hall in Denvash." Cael looked along the row at the lamp in the boarding-house window, because it was easier than looking up. "It's got my name on it, and the day, and one word, and the word isn't a Path. They didn't have a row to put me in. So they wrote down the nearest thing to nothing they had, and shut the book, and the next boy came up." He put his hands under his arms. "That was them deciding. It took about as long as it takes to say it. I'd rather take a year and get it right, from what I can actually see, than walk about wearing their word because they were quicker."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:291:"My family had a page on me," he said. "Not in a hall. At home, in the long room. Nobody wrote it down. They didn't need to; everybody had read it." He was quiet a moment. "It said I was a thing to be put right. Kindly. With good letters." The corner of his mouth went in. "I left before they'd finished the putting."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:297:"You read Vell's book from the front," said Cael. "Everybody else starts at the back, with the new names. Only somebody who grew up with a book on him reads one from the front, to see how it was kept." He shrugged. "I've been watching too."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:301:"Five days," said Brom. "Dace has put the bill up for the door. Main floor." He turned to go on up toward the north gate, and then turned back, as he did, with one shoulder already gone. "I've never fought anything I couldn't read. I want to see what I do."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:307:"Not mine," said Cael. "Hers."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:1:# Chapter 22 — The Shelf
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:23:Then she said something to Vell that he did not hear, and Vell said something back, and Lira turned on the stool and looked across the floor, not at Cael, but at Dace.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:31:*L. over Wendel (Iron-equiv., Wind, guild-trained). Called in the sixth. Took his right side in the first exchange and held it; made every sequence of his begin from the slow side; when he changed his feet in the third she changed back inside the same exchange. Long exchanges throughout. Her answers were to the man in front of her, not to the man of a minute ago.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:43:"She went back," said Lira. She turned the pages backward, slowly, with her good hand, through the month. Under each of her losses, in the margin, so small that he would not have seen it unless she had put her finger on it, Vell had made a mark: a short line and a figure, the figure of Thursday's page. "Every one. The clock. The Shield. The north-gate Wind. Every line it touched. She said she'd do that, once. To somebody. I heard her say it to your big man at the table, the day he read her book from the front, and I thought it was a thing people say."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:49:"No."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:61:"You'll have them after Tuesday." Dace looked out at the lane, where the sweepers were coming down the hill with their brooms over their shoulders. "One thing on my main floor at a time, and on Tuesday the thing is them." He tipped his head at Cael without looking at him. "After that I'll find you two. Good ones. Not anybody you'd beat on a cold night."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:73:*BROM. IRON SKIN. IRON-EQUIV.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:75:*CAEL. ASSESSED.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:97:"It was. About Lira."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:99:"Well." The betting man rubbed out a mark and wrote it again a little lower. "It's a good line. It cuts both ways, like most good lines."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:109:"The fruit woman's put a copper on the big one," she told Cael. "For luck, she says. She says she likes you better but she likes her copper best." He considered the slate. "Four to one's a lot."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:159:"Then I'll have the north mark," said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:175:"You kept them open," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:185:He looked across the alcove at Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:193:Cael filled the middle column for the last time. He did not need to. He had more than he could use. But he counted the gap through the first round and the second and the third, and wrote the beats down in a neat line, and saw the line do what it had done for twelve days: long, and then shorter, and then shorter, as the man got used to a thing. He wrote at the end of it, *Same. Every time.* It was a dull entry to finish on. He was glad of it. He had been afraid, a little, that on the last day the numbers would turn round and show him he had been wrong for twelve days, and they had not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:199:"You don't read people from the back of a room," said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:209:"It's part of the Path."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:211:"It came with it." Brom began on the right hand. "The same day. Fourteen."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:221:"Something I've never seen before," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:225:"Everybody's got a weight," he said. "Not the body. The other thing. The Path, and what the rank's done to it. I feel it the way you feel a man walking on the floor upstairs, through a beam. Every one I've stood near, I've kept. Like a shelf in a room. Blades on one shelf. Forces on another. Winds. Stone. The ones with nothing yet, who aren't kindled; they've a shelf too, an empty one." He moved one thumb on the cloth. "Two years, three cities. I stopped finding new shelves a long while back. Everything I stood near went on one I'd already got."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:229:"I've been along every shelf," said Brom. "You're not on one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:233:"Not empty," Brom went on, in the same flat, careful voice, as though he were reading a figure off a page and wanted to get every digit right. "I know empty. It's not that. And not wrong, the way a man's wrong when he's sick, or lying about his Path. It's weight where there oughtn't to be weight. Moving about. Never standing still in one place long enough to be a shape." He stopped, and seemed to go back over what he had said, and found it would do. "That's all. I haven't got a word for it. I don't think there is one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:247:"Yes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:257:"I don't like not knowing a thing," he said. "I never have. At home they used to say I'd take a clock apart to find out why it was late." He looked at his open hand. "But I've stood near that bench every day for twelve days, and I'd sooner have done that than anything I've done since the boat shed." The corner of his mouth went in. "So I don't know. Both."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:259:"Both," said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:269:"Good." He put his coat on, and stood a moment in the arch of the alcove, filling it, as he had on the first morning. "Neither will I."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:289:"Yes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:293:"It matters that he looked. Properly. With nothing in it for him." Cael pulled his collar up. "And it means it's not only the books. It's not only the registry being old and stupid. A man who builds his own way of seeing, honest, from the ground up, still can't find me on it." He looked at the lamps going down the row. "So nobody's going to hand it to me. What I am. There isn't a book with it in. I'm going to have to work it out on my own."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:295:"On your own," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:307:"You're not doing it by yourself," said Lira. "You never have been. You just write it all down in a book with one name on the front, so it looks like you are." She looked away down the row again. "I don't mind. I like the book. But don't sit on my step and tell me *on your own*, Cael. Not to me. It's rude."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:311:He thought about the grey book, and about every page in it, and tried to think of one that had nobody else in it. He could not. Dessa was in it, and the clock, and the river-academy man, and Keth, and Ulric, and Corrin with his thick knuckles, and Hesk's mill-wheel on a page he had thought was only about Brom. Lira was on nearly every page, mostly in the margin, mostly as *L.*, as though she were a measurement and not a person.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:313:"I'll put your name in it," he said. "Properly. Not *L.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:315:"Put it in ink," said Lira. "You never put anything in ink. I want to be the thing you can't rub out." She leaned her good shoulder against his, and left it there. "And put the big one's in too. He's earned it. *Brom*, in ink, with a shelf drawn round it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:319:"He'd pretend to hate it," said Lira. "And then he'd read it from the front."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:325:He went back first, through the whole of the two weeks, and wherever he had written *L.* he wrote *Lira* above it, small, in ink. It took a long time. There were more of them than he had thought, and some of them were on pages that had nothing to do with her at all, as if she had wandered into the margins of other people's fights. On the page about the middle column he wrote *Keth asked*, and on the coverage map he wrote *her partner*, and on the first page with Brom's name at the head of it, under *Iron Skin. Formal tier not known*, he wrote in ink, *Brom*, again, and then, because she had told him to and because it was true, a line round it with corners like a shelf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:331:*Tuesday. North mark. Third exchange: the fourth one low, into his recovery. I do it whatever the first two show me, unless they show me there's no recovery to go into. Deciding is the half beat, so I'm doing my deciding tonight.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-20.md:1:# Chapter 20 — Cheaper When Read
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-20.md:7:"Now forget it," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-20.md:41:*Claim: the cost of a burst isn't fixed. Depends on how ready I was when I asked, not only on how many I've done. Evidence: the Blade from Orvet's, ex 2 and ex 3; this morning, six marks, twenty and twenty, L. calling the late ones. Read first: thin line, breath back in four. Called late: wide line, breath back in eight or nine, knee twice, one short. Ruling: yes.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-20.md:51:"No."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-20.md:77:*PRESSURE-ADJACENT. Costs (add): giving face, three on the beat in one sequence. First two clean. Third thinner, scraped up. The hollow under the ribs grew with each one: egg, fist, hole. Breath ragged after the third. Didn't throw a fourth. Ruling: three is the working ceiling. It isn't the thing that fails past three. It's me. The face draws from the same place that keeps my breath in order, and a fight can't be fought with that place empty. Whatever's past three, a bout that counts is the wrong place to meet it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-20.md:81:He wrote that down too, underneath, because it was funny and true: *Ceiling for B.: nought. See the sheet.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-20.md:127:"Yesterday's," said Lira. "Half. Before the bell. You have to look poor."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-20.md:133:"No," said Lira, looking at him. "You really can't." She broke the end off a loaf and held it out to him. "Here. You can owe me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-20.md:141:"You're timing your pulse," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-20.md:155:"You'd be counting," said Brom. "Not noticing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-20.md:159:"It's not my pulse," said Cael. "It's the same hit, from the same boy. Your answer comes sooner. I've watched it twenty times. Whatever does it in you is getting used to it. It learns."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-20.md:167:"On me?" said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-20.md:189:"It's both," said Lira. "You're welcome."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-20.md:227:It happened without Cael. He came down the hill after the noon meal and found the broad man with the broken nose in the second alcove, with his coat off and his sleeves rolled to the elbow, and Brom standing in front of him with his hands wound, and Lira in the arch with her arms folded and an expression of great innocence.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-20.md:229:"He asked me," she said, before Cael could open his mouth. "This morning. At the post. He came across and stood in the arch and said, *Your partner. Does he hit hard.* That was it. Six words. I said yes. He said, *Can I borrow him.* I said he costs a mark an hour. He said, *Good.*" She unfolded her arms. "So I've lent him my partner for an hour, for a mark, and I've got the mark, and you've got something to watch that isn't a twelve-year-old."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-20.md:243:*Live. Dock partner, four in a run. Three held and thrown. Then the recovery: two beats, not half, and the breath in short and high. Fourth landed. He gave a step.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-20.md:249:Across the alcove Brom paid the dock partner a mark of his own, though Lira had already had one for him, and the dock partner looked at the coin and then at Brom and put it in his pocket. Brom sat down on his own bench and began to unwind his hands, slowly. He did not look at Cael. But when the left hand was free he flexed it open and shut, twice, and then sat with it open on his knee, palm up, as if he were letting something drain out of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-16.md:1:# Chapter 16 — Load First
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-16.md:27:"Usually." She dipped her pen. "Not always. When it doesn't, I say so, in front of the person, and I correct every line it touched. Which is more work than rating them right the first time, so I try to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-16.md:55:He had not gone and spoken to him. He had written *I owe him a conversation. When he's ready* at the bottom of a page, and he had meant the second half of it as much as the first. In three weeks he had not once seen the man ready for anything. He had seen him at the back by the water barrel on two thin cards, the broad still shape on the last bench that Cael had once counted as part of the furniture. And at his own bout the week before, against a Shield from the salt end, he had seen him somewhere much nearer: at the rope by the north post, close, with his forearms folded on the top strand. He had not watched the Shield at all. He had watched Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-16.md:61:"He's been at her book an hour," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-16.md:65:"From the back," said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-16.md:77:At the bottom of the page he was working on, without quite meaning to, he wrote a single line with no name above it. *Big. Stands at the rope. Doesn't watch the fight. Watches me.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-16.md:85:"No."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-16.md:109:Gold ran down through the family the way the drive ran down under the old trees. His grandmother's card said Gold, and so did his mother's, though if you went by the figures and not the card his mother sat nearer the top of Silver than the bottom of Gold. Nobody in the house had ever said so; the card said Gold, and the card was what people saw. His sister was two years older than he was and already two years into a guild placement in a city by the sea, and she was spoken of at the long table in the voice the family kept for a thing that was going to go well. Nobody in three generations of that house had been read at a station and come out anywhere but at the top of the ladder, or near enough to it that nobody mentioned the gap over the soup.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-16.md:111:His own had come out Iron Skin, Copper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-16.md:171:The read was part of the Path. It had come with the hardness at fourteen, in the same breath. It was a sense of the other person's weight, and not the weight of their body only. He had never found a way to say what it was like that satisfied him. The nearest was the beams of an old house: when you sat in a room with your hand flat on the timber, you could feel who was moving on the floor above you. You felt a heavy step or a light one, a child running, a man carrying something and setting it down. Every practitioner pressed on that sense a little, in a shape their Path gave them and their rank sharpened. A Blade was a thin hard line leaning forward. A Force was a slow loading, like a cart being filled sack by sack, and then a crack. A Wind was weight that was there on one board and then, with no step between, on the next. The unkindled were boards with nobody on them. In two years and three circuits he had felt so many shapes that he had stopped expecting new ones.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-16.md:191:Dace did not stop chalking. "He's Assessed. Copper-equivalent in her book, and a few of those above it, if you look at what he's beaten."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-16.md:201:"You're Iron-equivalent," said Dace. "In her book."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-16.md:203:"Yes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md:1:# Chapter 18 — Stimulus, Latency, Response
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md:29:He had thought, coming up the dyers' stair, that he was going to read about a Path. He found he had read about a door instead, and the people who kept the key to it, and how long they had kept it. That was what the whole ladder was, he supposed, if you looked at it from far enough off: Copper, Iron, Bronze, and the guild letter, and the station, and a book in a hall with your name in it. It was four pages on who owned you and a sentence on what you could do.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md:41:"You're asking round about Iron Skin."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md:57:"Like a gatepost. Back. Way back, on her heels, like somebody'd planted her and come back in a year to see if she'd taken." He snorted. "I thought it was a fault. I thought, she can't go anywhere from there. I was going to make her chase me. She didn't chase. Why would she?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md:65:The second man Cael asked was a Blade from Orvet's gym who had lost to an Iron Skin in the second city, and he wanted mostly to talk about the injustice of it, and about a keeper who had scored it wrong, and about his own left knee. Cael listened politely for as long as he could and wrote down one word, *knee*, which turned out to be about the man and not the Path.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md:87:Cael's page, at the end of the first hour, read: *Dormant. Dormant. Hit, left forearm, boy. Dormant. Hit, chest, girl. Dormant.* It read like a man writing *still shut* outside a shut door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md:95:Across the alcove Brom paid off the tall lad, and sat down on his own bench, and began to unwind the cloth from his hands. He did not look at Cael. But as he finished the left hand and started on the right, he said, to the cloth, "Anything?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md:97:"No."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md:125:That would do. It went faster when he was frightened, but he was not frightened of Brom. It went faster when he ran, but he would be sitting still. He counted it against the shadow of the lamp-flame moving on the wall, for long enough to trust it. Then he wrote *pulse* at the foot of the middle column, and went to bed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md:133:"I've never listened to it." He let go of his wrist. "I need to know what one beat is. Exactly. So I can say a thing took one and a half."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md:173:"Everybody watches the hit," Keth said at last. "Or the answer, if they've any sense. Nobody watches the bit in the middle." He turned back to the ring. "That's a better thing to count than most of what you count. I've seen your book." And then, as Cael turned to go, without looking round: "Tell me what's in it. The middle. After."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md:213:*Heels. Six in ten, more. Can't move off it — tried. Not a fault. The floor is part of it. Whatever he sends back goes through his heels first.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-17.md:1:# Chapter 17 — Two Weeks
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-17.md:9:"Brom. Iron Skin Path. We fight two weeks from today."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-17.md:33:"Good. Two weeks."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-17.md:45:"No."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-17.md:51:"No. He saves them up and buys you with them." She looked across at the second alcove, where the big man had finished one hand and begun the other. "He's going to train there."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-17.md:63:*Brom.* He wrote the name at the head. Under it, *Iron Skin. Formal tier not known. Iron-equivalent in Vell's book* (Dace would not clear the main floor for less, and Dace had chalked it at the top of the slate in yellow before the sweepers were done). Under that went the whole of what he knew about Iron Skin. It came to four lines, and he did not need to look anything up for any of them. It was a Path, filed with the defensive ones in every list he had ever seen. It made the body hard, or parts of it. Whoever trained it kept the rest to themselves, and nobody outside had any reason to know.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-17.md:81:He looked at the second sentence for a long time. Every honest sum he could run said it. He did not know Iron Skin. He did not know this man. He had been watched by him for three weeks and more, from close, while he had watched nothing back. The calm arithmetic he ran on every bout before he agreed to it came out, this time, on the other side of the line, and he had never once made a bout go better by lying to the page about it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-17.md:89:"Twice. The first one." She broke the end off the loaf and gave it to him. "You think you're looking forward to it. You *think* you'll lose. You're hedging both of them, and you never hedge." She sat down and broke off a piece for herself. "You've gone soft on him already."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-17.md:93:"You've met him. Ninety-odd words." She chewed. "That's more than he's said to anybody else in this building. I asked Vell."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-17.md:107:They were short, even for Vell. Each one had the man's name, his Path, the exchange in which it ended. Under every one of the seven, in the narrow upright hand, there was the same thing in slightly different words, as if she had tried each time to find a new way to say it and each time had come back to the old one. *Took everything; gave nothing first.* *Did not begin.* *Opponent's own blows.* *Stood.* Under the fifth win, against an Iron-equivalent Force from the tannery end, she had written only *Stood*, and then, a little apart from it, in a smaller hand: *Force retired with the right wrist.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-17.md:113:It was a loss. It had been Brom's first bout on that stone, two months back, against a Shield from the river end whom Cael knew slightly, a careful heavy fighter who did nothing quickly. *B. to the Shield (Iron-equiv.). Seventh exchange. Hand up.* And under it, in Vell's hand, three words.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-17.md:123:He turned the book back round and pushed it to Vell.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-17.md:135:"Yes." She went back to her page. "It's a good one. He learned the floor in three weeks. Remember that, before you go thinking you've found him out. He's had a good deal longer than three weeks to learn you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-17.md:137:Cael went back to the bench and wrote it under Brom's name, under *Iron Skin. Formal tier not known*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-17.md:139:*One loss, first bout, the Shield. "Did not know the floor." Then three weeks learning it, hand on the stone. Then seven wins, nothing given first. He learns places before he fights in them. He's been learning me for longer than that.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-17.md:149:"One," said Lira. "Same as ever."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-17.md:157:"Breathe first," said Lira. "Like I told you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-17.md:171:"No."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-17.md:173:"Once. To know."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-17.md:181:"Stop," said Lira, from somewhere. "Stop. Breathe. That's it. Stop."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-17.md:191:*WIND-ADJACENT. Conditions (add): chain of two. Fill first, past need; spend half on the first burst; hold the rest through the lock; spend it as the lock lets go. Six in seven tonight. Claim: repeatable. Evidence: seen, L. counting. Ruling: yes. Costs (add): the fill shows. Chest up before I go. A new tell, and a big one. Chain of three: not there. Asked once to know. Grey, ringing, knees. Ruling: no. Don't ask again to find out what I already know.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-17.md:207:The return was very short, in a young man's level hand, with every letter the same height as its neighbour. *Visit made. Subject found in district, morning, at the market square. Address confirmed by keeper's book. Compliant. Nothing further.* Below it, as the standing instructions had lately required on every file of that class: *Supplementary marker entered on closure, as instructed.* And the initials, *H.*, and a date a fortnight gone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-17.md:209:Havel. Coss knew him from the duty board: four years in, new boots in the corridor, a careful one whom nobody talked about because there was nothing to say.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-17.md:227:There were three lines in it now. The first was the registry's, *[SHATTERED]*, with its stamp. The third was the young assessor's green slip, entered in that level hand, with *H.* and the date in the right-hand column. Between them was a second line, in the short printed form the registry used for handling markers, a designation and a figure; and the column at its right-hand end, where an officer's initials went, was empty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-17.md:233:The query form was in his drawer upstairs. He thought of the last one he had sent, months ago, and the single word that had come back in the spring pouch in a hand he did not know: *Noted.* He thought of the kitchen table at home, and a girl doing her sums with her tongue between her teeth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-19.md:1:# Chapter 19 — Leave It Home
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-19.md:7:He began with the Wind. He had had it longest, and he leaned on it hardest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-19.md:19:He made himself fill in the fields for it as if he were writing the entry for the first time, Claim and Evidence and Ruling under each, with the small *h* beside anything he had only been told. *Deployment range*: half a body, the fan, nothing to the right-rear quarter. *Conditions, against B.*: a man who stands on his heels and waits, so that nothing ever comes at me past its hinge unless he wants it to. *Evidence*: four days of the middle column. *Ruling*: the conditions the burst was built for will hardly ever be there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-19.md:45:"Tomorrow," she said. "Early. Before your big man's hour. I want the alcove to ourselves."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-19.md:59:She was being Brom. Not well; she did not have the weight for it, and she did not have whatever it was that sent his hits back. But she had the shape: a fighter who began nothing and waited to answer. And she had a year of reading Cael from three paces off, which Brom had had for three weeks.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-19.md:67:"There," said Lira. "Again."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-19.md:79:"You told me something once," she said. "Last winter. You said a loss wasn't a loss if you got something out of it. Fine. Here's something out of your last four." She held up her hand and folded the fingers down. "Shoulder. Ribs. Shoulder. Knee. Every one of them, you were thinking about your hip. The step's been doing your thinking for a year, and the boy who was there before it hasn't been asked anything since." She dropped the hand. "He was good, you know. That boy. He came over the hills with a bag and nothing in his hip at all, and he stood at the canvas post at the Cinder House, and Renn knocked him down and Dessa knocked him down, and every time he got up having learned something. He didn't have a fragment to his name for three months. He kept himself alive in this district all that while on his feet and his eyes and nothing else."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-19.md:83:"I've got a Wind on Thursday's card," she said, to the post rather than to him. "Dace put me down last night. Iron-equivalent, guild-trained, a pin on his collar." She looked at the end of her staff. "I've watched him. Myself. I don't want your page."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-19.md:93:"There," said Lira. "That's him." She did not hit him. She lowered her staff. "Again."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-19.md:97:*L.: leave it home. You can't half-carry it. The deciding not to costs more than the doing. Find the one who was there before.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-19.md:101:After that he could not sleep, so he wrote to Hesk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-19.md:129:"Begin," said Vell.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-19.md:157:"Called," said Vell. "Hand up. Third exchange."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-19.md:163:"In the second," he said. "You went somewhere. Sideways. It looked like it hurt."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-19.md:169:Cael thought about telling him. It would have been easy, and the Blade had asked honestly, and there was nothing in it that would cost Cael anything on Tuesday. But he did not have it yet. He had one bout's worth of it, and a feeling, and one bout's worth of a thing said out loud to a stranger had a way of turning into something you believed.
