editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-17.md:1:# Chapter 17 — Two Weeks
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:13:He had known she would ask, and he had the page open already. He turned it round on his knee so she could read it over his shoulder, which she did, slowly, with her lips moving.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:23:"He breathes while I'm still coming. If he can see me, he's in before I get there." Cael turned back a page to the middle column. "And the hold's no good to me. By then he's already got my hit, and he's only deciding where to send it, and every time he's seen a thing he decides quicker. The getting back is the only one of the three that gets slower the more he does. Everything else, he gets better at."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:27:"If I'm right. I've seen it once, live." He turned forward again. "So I make him answer hard three times, close together, and put the fourth in the recovery." He moved his finger to the drawing. "Low. Below the knee. That's where he's thinnest anyway. If I'm right about the recovery and right about the knee, the fourth goes into a man who's only a man there, for two beats."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:29:Lira read the drawing for a long time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:33:"Not the first exchange. In the first he's slowest, because everything's new, but he's also got a full purse, and he's watching me to learn me. Not the second, either. I'll use the first two to make him answer. Different things each time, so he can't get used to any of them. Hard enough that he has to send them back." He turned the page. "Then the third. By the third he's settled into what he thinks I am. And he's spent some."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:65:Lira read it, and then read it again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:71:"You've got two things, and you've put one of them away before you've even walked onto the floor." She looked at him with something in her face he could not read. "And the other one's insurance."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:155:"This morning. At the post." Brom did not look away from the floor. "She said, *Don't read me tonight. I don't want to know what I look like from in there.*" He considered the floor. "So I'm standing here, and I'm not."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:157:"Can you not? If you're this close?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:169:That was the first thing, and Cael did not understand it at first. She did not come straight at him, nor to his left, where his back foot was and where a Wind's step was easiest to follow. She came in close on his right, closer than she needed, so that her shoulder was nearly at his elbow, and she stayed there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:179:In the second she did it again. She came in on his right and stayed there, so close that he could not begin a sequence where he liked to begin it, and every time he tried, the sequence had to come round from the left instead. And on the left it came slower.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:183:It was on his page. It was the line on his page, the one he had not given her. *Off side primes slow. His sequences build from the right; from the left the first part comes half a beat behind, every time, whatever he does.* He had seen it in the summer, in Wendel's second Ironyard bout, and written it down, and underlined it, and tonight he had kept his hand off his pocket on purpose so as not to give it to her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:185:She had not needed it. She was not waiting for the slow side. She was making him use it. Every sequence he began, she had already made begin on the side where he was late. She came in on his right and stood there and dared him, and when the left began, half a beat behind, she was already inside it, with her staff coming. Once, twice, the staff caught him, on the hip and the ribs. Neither was a finishing blow, but they were the first clean hits anybody had put on Wendel in the Ironyard, and the room made a different sound.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:209:Each time Wendel found something new, it came an exchange late, an answer to the Lira who had been in front of him a minute ago. Each time she answered him, it was the Lira in front of him now. Cael had won a dozen bouts in his own year off exactly that gap, from the inside, without ever being able to see it. From the rope he could see it as plainly as a crack in a plate, and see how Wendel's face changed as he felt it widening.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:235:It was an old habit, he knew, from the first months, when she had asked him things with her eyes before she asked anybody else. He knew too, standing at the rope with his hand on the strand, that whatever he did with his face now she would carry into her answer. So he did nothing with it. He kept it still, not the still face that had bits, but a face with nothing in it at all for her to read, and he left her to it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:243:Wendel came across the circle to Lira on his own feet, with his hand pressed to his ribs, and stood in front of her. Up close he was even taller than he had looked from the rope. He reached up, slowly, and unpinned the small enamelled thing from his collar, and looked at it in his palm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:253:"In the morning," said Vell. "Come and read it in the morning. Not now, with your arm like that and half the room watching you." She looked at Lira over her spectacles. "I want you to read it sitting down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:291:"My family had a page on me," he said. "Not in a hall. At home, in the long room. Nobody wrote it down. They didn't need to; everybody had read it." He was quiet a moment. "It said I was a thing to be put right. Kindly. With good letters." The corner of his mouth went in. "I left before they'd finished the putting."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:297:"You read Vell's book from the front," said Cael. "Everybody else starts at the back, with the new names. Only somebody who grew up with a book on him reads one from the front, to see how it was kept." He shrugged. "I've been watching too."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:301:"Five days," said Brom. "Dace has put the bill up for the door. Main floor." He turned to go on up toward the north gate, and then turned back, as he did, with one shoulder already gone. "I've never fought anything I couldn't read. I want to see what I do."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md:19:The register was laid out the way the Compact laid out everything, in rows. He had read its front pages before, in the spring, when he was trying to understand the ladder he had been put at the bottom of. There was the table of the metals, Copper and Iron and Bronze and Silver and Gold, with ten ranks to each and a line under the tenth where the station had to look at you again. There was the list of what each metal let you do and where it let you go: Copper the outer districts and the limited market; Iron the whole city, and the guild district with your credentials in your hand; Bronze the administrative core as well, where the gates were. And after the front pages came the Paths themselves, each one a row, with its branch and the metals at which anybody had ever been recorded holding it, and a line or two of what it did. There was no row in it for anybody who had not been given a Path, and he had stopped looking for one a long time ago.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md:23:He read the four pages twice, because he did not at first believe them. They were about who owned it. Here were the three guild houses that taught it, and when each had been founded and by whom. Here was which of them had quarrelled with which, and which families had sent their children to which house for four generations, and what those children had become. One page was nothing but a list of marriages. And in the middle of the second page, between the founding of one house and the founding of the next, there was a single sentence on what the Path did. *Its practitioners raise a hardness of the surface of the body at need, the art of which is the property of the houses named above.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md:25:He copied the sentence out exactly into the grey book. Under it he wrote: *Four pages on who owns it. One sentence on what it does. The registry's the same, only shorter.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md:29:He had thought, coming up the dyers' stair, that he was going to read about a Path. He found he had read about a door instead, and the people who kept the key to it, and how long they had kept it. That was what the whole ladder was, he supposed, if you looked at it from far enough off: Copper, Iron, Bronze, and the guild letter, and the station, and a book in a hall with your name in it. It was four pages on who owned you and a sentence on what you could do.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md:45:"Because of the big one." Corrin did not wait for an answer. "I fought one once. Years back, in a port up the coast, before your mother had you. A woman, not big. Iron Skin, Iron tier, and she looked at me like I was a horse she was thinking of buying." He worked his right hand open and shut on the head of his stick. The knuckles were thick, and two of them did not close all the way. "I'll tell you what it's like and you can make of it what you want. It's not her hitting you. She hardly hit me at all. It's your own hit. You put your best into her, everything you've got, and the hit comes back up your arm the wrong way, on a line you didn't send it on. Your wrist knows before your eyes do. Like punching water that decided, halfway, to be a wall."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md:87:Cael's page, at the end of the first hour, read: *Dormant. Dormant. Hit, left forearm, boy. Dormant. Hit, chest, girl. Dormant.* It read like a man writing *still shut* outside a shut door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md:119:And over the middle column, the one he had never had a use for, the one that had always been only the space between his other columns, he wrote *Latency*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md:161:"Three days of hitting a man who sends it back at her," said Keth, "and she's stopped being frightened of the stick. I couldn't teach her that in a month. I tried." He said it plainly, without any sourness, the way he said everything, but Cael saw his little finger close on the grip and then lift off it again. "You can't tell somebody it's only a stick. They have to find out it comes back and they're still standing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md:183:From the benches, a newcomer's fist on Brom's forearm looked like one thing: the knock, and the newcomer flung away. From close, with his fingers on his wrist, it was two things with a stillness between them. The knock came first. Then the fist stayed where it had landed, stopped dead against the arm as if it had hit a gatepost, and did not bounce, while Brom's knees gave a finger's width and the weight of the blow went down through him into his heels, the way Hesk's water went into the gears before the wheel turned. Then the arm turned, and the newcomer went off sideways with his own fist flung wide. That stillness, from the knock to the throw, was what Cael counted, and he called it the hold.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md:185:On the first hit of a kind the hold ran close to a whole beat of his pulse. When the same fist came the same way a second time, and a third, from the same newcomer, the hold shortened toward half a beat and sometimes less, as if Brom spent it deciding where to send a thing and needed less time to decide once he had sent that thing somewhere before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md:191:It meant that Brom would be slowest with anything new and quicker with everything he had seen, so that a man who wanted the hold to stay long would have to keep showing him new things. It also meant, Cael noticed, writing it, that the hold was no use to anybody: by the time it began, Brom already had the hit.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:11:"I mean it. If you're at the table I'll look at your face before I've read it, and then I'll have read your face and not the book." She pushed the door. "I'm doing this one without you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:13:The sweepers had not come yet. The floor was grey and empty and smelled of last night's lamp oil and the cold coming up off the stone. Vell was already at her table, as she always was, with the book shut in front of her and a second stool set at the end of the table, the side nearest the door. Cael had never seen a second stool at Vell's table. Nobody sat at Vell's table. People stood at the end of it and read, and went away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:21:It took her a long time. It took her long enough to read it three times, which Cael thought she had, because her finger went back to the top twice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:31:*L. over Wendel (Iron-equiv., Wind, guild-trained). Called in the sixth. Took his right side in the first exchange and held it; made every sequence of his begin from the slow side; when he changed his feet in the third she changed back inside the same exchange. Long exchanges throughout. Her answers were to the man in front of her, not to the man of a minute ago.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:41:Cael read the last part twice, as Lira had, and then sat looking at it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:43:"She went back," said Lira. She turned the pages backward, slowly, with her good hand, through the month. Under each of her losses, in the margin, so small that he would not have seen it unless she had put her finger on it, Vell had made a mark: a short line and a figure, the figure of Thursday's page. "Every one. The clock. The Shield. The north-gate Wind. Every line it touched. She said she'd do that, once. To somebody. I heard her say it to your big man at the table, the day he read her book from the front, and I thought it was a thing people say."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:47:"It's a thing Vell does." Lira shut the book and held it on her knees with both hands, the way she had held the wrist cloth the morning Brom first came to the alcove. "I asked her what was in the back cover. What she wrote, after the Dravin month. You know what she said?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:69:The bill was on the outside of the plank door by the middle of the morning, and by noon half the district had read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:77:Cael stood in the lane and read it with the people going past him. He had seen his name chalked on the slate a hundred times. He had never seen it on the outside of the door before, where the street could read it, and it looked different there, smaller and more exposed, like a coat hung out to dry.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:89:"You don't lose to people who come at you," he said. "Everybody knows that. You've beaten every man on that slate who goes first." He tapped the long column with his chalk. "But you've lost to men who wait. Not many, and not lately, but the money's got a long memory and it remembers the spring. And your big man doesn't wait. He doesn't even start." He shrugged. "The money's seen a boy who wins by reading, against a man who's spent three weeks letting you read him, and it's decided that a man who lets you read him has a reason. Money's not clever. But it's honest about what it's seen."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:101:While they stood there, the river-academy man came up the lane from the river end with his coat buttoned to his chin. He did not stop to read the bill; he had read it already, by the look of him. He went to the betting man, and took a single coin out of his pocket, and laid it on the crate on the side of the short column, and waited while the betting man wrote it. Then he nodded to Cael, the small left-handed nod, and went on up the lane without a word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:119:He had watched a hundred bouts on it from the east bench, with somebody else's name in yellow at the top of the slate, and he had always been too busy reading the fighters to read the stone. Now the floor was empty, and the Stone pair's posts were coming out of their sockets to be carried to the side floor, and the big square of old foundry stone in the middle of the Ironyard lay bare under the high windows with the morning on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:155:And under it, smaller: *Take the mark with open room to my left, so the fan has somewhere to go if I'm ever made to use it. Ask Dace which mark is mine.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:193:Cael filled the middle column for the last time. He did not need to. He had more than he could use. But he counted the gap through the first round and the second and the third, and wrote the beats down in a neat line, and saw the line do what it had done for twelve days: long, and then shorter, and then shorter, as the man got used to a thing. He wrote at the end of it, *Same. Every time.* It was a dull entry to finish on. He was glad of it. He had been afraid, a little, that on the last day the numbers would turn round and show him he had been wrong for twelve days, and they had not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:199:"You don't read people from the back of a room," said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:203:"You sat at the back for a month, by the water barrel. I counted you there. Then you came down and sat on the end of my bench, close enough to touch my sleeve, and you sat there two exchanges and went. Then three weeks at the rope. Not the back bench. The rope." Cael closed the grey book on his finger. "And now you sit there, a bench's length off, every day, and I've watched you not look at me. It's close, whatever you do. It doesn't reach far."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:215:"What do you see?" he said at last, because the waiting had gone on long enough to be its own kind of asking. "When you're close. When it's me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:237:He thought of the brown register in the reading room over the dyers' hall, a hundred years old, with a row in it for every Path that anybody had ever been recorded holding, and no row for anybody who had not been given one. He thought of a hall in Denvash, and a clerk's flat voice, and a word written down because there was nothing else to write. For a year he had believed that the trouble was the books. The books were old, and kept by people who owned the key, and had been built in rows by people who had only ever seen what fitted in a row. Now here was a man who had built his own book out of nothing but standing close to people, without a registry or an almanac or a guild, out of his own body, honestly, for two years. And his book had no shelf for him either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:245:"I know. I read your line in her book. *Assessed*." Brom looked at him for the first time since the cloth had stopped. "That's a word for *we looked and didn't find a word*."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:303:"Who stood on her heels for two hours being a man she'd never fought, so you'd stop reaching for your hip?" She did not wait. "Whose dock partner showed you the gap? Who writes to you from Denvash every month to tell you to eat? Whose book do you read your own name in every morning, that's just this week written down that she was wrong, so you'd know the rest of it's right?" She turned and looked at him. "And who sat on a bench across from you for twelve days, letting you count him, and then told you what he saw when you asked him, when he didn't have to and it didn't win him anything?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:319:"He'd pretend to hate it," said Lira. "And then he'd read it from the front."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:335:*Wrong if he takes my first two soft and spends nothing. He chooses which hits he answers hard; I've watched him choose. Wrong if the main floor, with the whole room on him, makes him fill quicker than he does in an alcove. Wrong if the shin isn't bare, only untested, and the tall lad tested it for me today in front of him. Wrong if twelve days of me watching have taught him where I'll be looking.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:337:*If any of those, I'll know by the end of the third, and I'll have four exchanges left to read why. A loss to the right man, watched all the way through, is still worth buying.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:341:But he did not think Brom had chosen. He thought of the column itself: twelve days of numbers that had never once jumped where a lie would have made them jump, but had gone long and then short in the same plain curve every afternoon, the way a thing does when nobody is steering it. And he thought of the big man on the corner of the row saying *My family had a page on me*, which was not a thing a man said to somebody he meant to cheat. None of that was proof. It was what Brom was made of, read the only way Cael had of reading it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-17.md:39:He did not wait to be answered. He went off across the floor to the second alcove along, where nobody trained in the mornings, and took his coat off and hung it on a nail. Then he sat down on the bench there and began, slowly and without any hurry at all, to wind a strip of cloth round his left hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-17.md:67:*Sat on the end of the east bench in my ten degrees for two exchanges and I didn't see him come. Luck, or he knows what my eyes cost me when I go deep. Read my method off my pencil, not my fighting. Then three weeks at the rope, close: watched me, not the floor. Saw the book change from the rope and knew what it meant.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-17.md:71:*He does what I do. From a floor further down.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-17.md:81:He looked at the second sentence for a long time. Every honest sum he could run said it. He did not know Iron Skin. He did not know this man. He had been watched by him for three weeks and more, from close, while he had watched nothing back. The calm arithmetic he ran on every bout before he agreed to it came out, this time, on the other side of the line, and he had never once made a bout go better by lying to the page about it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-17.md:103:Vell was writing up the morning's sparring for the newcomers, which she did not have to do and did anyway, and she pushed the book across to him without looking up. He stood at the end of her table where the big man had stood the day before, and found Brom's name, and read him from the back, the newest line first, the way everybody read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-17.md:109:Cael read the seven twice. They told him what Corrin's knuckles had told him, and what the shut door of the alcove had told him: that nobody who had fought this man had been able to make him do anything at all. It was useful in the way a wall is useful to know about. It was not a map.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-17.md:201:A contact of record kept his name on a file long after his visits were finished, for the convenience of somebody's register, so that every time a district assessor went out on it a copy of the return came up two floors to his desk, *for information*. Information meant nothing was asked of him. He read the head, put his thumb on the routing card, and signed nothing, a dozen times a week.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-17.md:215:The green slip was the Tuesday instruction, a few weeks old; every file of that class had to carry one now. Coss had read the instruction on his own floor and wondered for the length of one breath who had wanted it, and then stopped wondering, because wondering about instructions was a thing a man could only do for so long before it showed in his face.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-17.md:231:Under nobody's line, the young man had signed his own. Havel would have read the leaf from top to bottom, as anybody who wrote that hand read everything, and seen the empty column when he came to enter his line beneath it, and he had not taken it upstairs. He had written *as instructed* and closed the file. Coss knew exactly what that had cost him, because it was what Coss had done; and he felt two things at once, neither of which he liked. One was relief, plain and shameful, that the young man had sent no note upstairs with his name on it. The other was the thought that he had now watched two men find the same nothing in the same file and say nothing, and that one of them had learned it from the other without ever being told.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-19.md:21:*Against him,* he wrote, *the burst is insurance. Not a weapon. Keep it for the one time he does come, if he comes. Don't spend it looking for a reason to.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-19.md:33:*Every hit I put into him, he sends back. That's the whole Path, as far as I can see it. A hardness at the place I touch, at the instant I touch, and my own hit back up my arm on a line I didn't send it on. The giving face makes my hit heavier. So every time I use it on him, I'm paying out of my own middle to make the hit that comes back at me bigger.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-19.md:43:Lira came down at the end of it for a cup of water and read the sheet upside down over his shoulder, the whole of it, without a word. Then she drank her water standing at the table, looking at the wall, and put the cup down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-19.md:69:It happened again. He went in, and round, and his hip leaned toward the step he had promised not to use, and he stopped it, and in the stopping she hit him. Shoulder, ribs, shoulder again. The fourth time it was the side of his knee, which hurt more than it needed to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-19.md:109:*Dear Hesk,* he wrote at last. *I have a big bout on Tuesday week against a man I can't read yet, and I'm not going to win it with anything I've learned since I got here. Lira says I have to leave the new things at home and fight with what I brought. So I've been trying to remember what I brought, and I find I don't know. I know what I carried. I had the bag and the first notebook and the clothes I stood up in. I don't know what the boy carrying them was like. You'd know. I'm not asking you to tell me, because the letter won't get to you before Tuesday, and anyway I think I'm meant to find out on the floor. I only wanted you to know I'm looking for him.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-19.md:111:He read it over. It was not the kind of letter he wrote. His letters to Hesk were lists, mostly, because Hesk liked lists. They held what things cost, who had won, what the river was doing. This had no list in it at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-19.md:115:Then he folded it and put it with the other, unfinished one on the shelf, and lay down. It would reach Denvash in about a week. Whatever happened on the main floor would have happened by then, and Hesk would read the letter at the kitchen table knowing nothing of it, in the twenty minutes of sideways light, and Cael would be the only one of them who knew how it had come out. He thought that there was something fair in that, though he could not have said what, and on the thought he slept.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-19.md:131:The Blade went at the word, as the page said, with a shout that made two people on the bench behind Lira jump. Cael went with his feet, as Lira had taught him that morning, and the first exchange was all feet: the Blade coming and coming, cut on cut, each one off the front foot and each one with the shout behind it, and Cael giving ground and turning, giving ground and turning, round the edge of the circle with the chalk line a hand's breadth from his heels. Twice the Blade's cut came close enough that Cael felt the wind of it on his cheek, and once it took a strip off the shoulder of his shirt, and Orvet's four roared.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-19.md:133:He did not burst. He felt the hip lean, twice, the old pull, and he let it lean and did not answer it, and the deciding cost him as Lira had said it would: on the second time, half a beat of nothing that the Blade's next cut very nearly filled. He had decided, in the morning, that he would burst in this bout only when he had read the hinge first, and never otherwise, because he wanted to know something. It was harder to keep to than it had been to decide.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-19.md:145:He walked to his mark with his lungs pulling and his hip shouting and thought, very clearly, *that was a reaction*. He had not read it. It had found him, and he had answered it from the bottom of himself, crooked, with nothing set.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-19.md:177:Vell turned the book for him, and kept her thumb over the Blade's name, and he read *…Third exchange. Atypical movement pattern.* She had begun blotting it before he had finished.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-19.md:201:*Same step. Same distance. Half the price, when I'd read it first. The reading isn't a different thing from the burst. It might be what pays for it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-16.md:5:"You read it from the back."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-16.md:13:There were five names in the book that he knew from other rooms. Three of them he had fought in the river city in his first winter away, and two in the second city in the spring after. He found all five and read every line under every one of them, and then he stood a while with his thumb in the page and thought about what he had found.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-16.md:55:He had not gone and spoken to him. He had written *I owe him a conversation. When he's ready* at the bottom of a page, and he had meant the second half of it as much as the first. In three weeks he had not once seen the man ready for anything. He had seen him at the back by the water barrel on two thin cards, the broad still shape on the last bench that Cael had once counted as part of the furniture. And at his own bout the week before, against a Shield from the salt end, he had seen him somewhere much nearer: at the rope by the north post, close, with his forearms folded on the top strand. He had not watched the Shield at all. He had watched Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-16.md:57:Cael had won that bout in the third exchange. He had felt the man's attention on him the whole time, steady and patient and close, like the warmth of a stove at his back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-16.md:71:"You keep saying that. What does it look like? Him being ready?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-16.md:81:Lira had come up behind him without his hearing, which she could still do when she wanted to, and read it over his shoulder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-16.md:89:"It's a note that he's close." He shut the book. "From the barrel you can't see anything. He's moved in. He's been moving in for three weeks."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-16.md:109:Gold ran down through the family the way the drive ran down under the old trees. His grandmother's card said Gold, and so did his mother's, though if you went by the figures and not the card his mother sat nearer the top of Silver than the bottom of Gold. Nobody in the house had ever said so; the card said Gold, and the card was what people saw. His sister was two years older than he was and already two years into a guild placement in a city by the sea, and she was spoken of at the long table in the voice the family kept for a thing that was going to go well. Nobody in three generations of that house had been read at a station and come out anywhere but at the top of the ladder, or near enough to it that nobody mentioned the gap over the soup.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-16.md:113:He had stood in the station's bare room under the Arbiter with his mother's hand on his shoulder, and heard it read out in the clerk's flat voice. Iron Skin was a Path filed among the defensive ones, the kind that kept you whole. He was Copper, the first metal, with ten ranks to climb before anybody would look at him again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-16.md:117:His family had not been sorry, exactly. Nobody had been cruel; he had gone over that afterward many times, and it was true. There had been no shouting and no cold dinners. There had been, instead, a great many quiet conversations in the long room, some of which he was part of and most of which he was not. They were about training, and about which instructor might be got to see to it. A man had come to the house within the month, with very good letters, whose work it was to correct what had gone short. They talked about the station their friends kept, three days' ride off, and a good day to be read again on, after a year of the right work, so that the next reading would be better.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-16.md:169:That was where the trouble began. From the back by the barrel, Brom could not read him at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-16.md:171:The read was part of the Path. It had come with the hardness at fourteen, in the same breath. It was a sense of the other person's weight, and not the weight of their body only. He had never found a way to say what it was like that satisfied him. The nearest was the beams of an old house: when you sat in a room with your hand flat on the timber, you could feel who was moving on the floor above you. You felt a heavy step or a light one, a child running, a man carrying something and setting it down. Every practitioner pressed on that sense a little, in a shape their Path gave them and their rank sharpened. A Blade was a thin hard line leaning forward. A Force was a slow loading, like a cart being filled sack by sack, and then a crack. A Wind was weight that was there on one board and then, with no step between, on the next. The unkindled were boards with nobody on them. In two years and three circuits he had felt so many shapes that he had stopped expecting new ones.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-16.md:173:But it reached only a little way. It was a close sense, the length of a bench and not much more, and from the back of the room the boy was a boy and nothing else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-16.md:175:So one night he had moved down to the end of the east bench, where nobody sat, while the boy was deep in whatever he did with his eyes. He sat close enough to touch his sleeve, and laid his attention on him like a hand on a beam.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-16.md:183:He had stood there at both of the boy's bouts in that time, close, with his forearms on the top strand, and read him while he fought, and on the nights between he had sat at the near end of whatever bench the boy was not on. In the fights it moved. Once, as the boy stepped short off his left hip in a way no legs did, there was a flicker of something very like Wind, there and gone. Once, longer, there was a density gathering into his arm that read for a moment almost like Pressure, and then did not. They came and went, now one shape and now another, and none of them stood the way a Path stands. They were like several weights moving on the floor above at once, none of them a whole person, all of them going round some middle point he could feel the pull of and could not find.
